From: mditto@google.com (Mike Ditto)
Subject: [PATCH] forcedeth: Fix different hardware statistics versions.
Date: Thu, 22 Jul 2010 15:02:52 -0700 (PDT)
Message-ID: <20100722220252.E46C7182B00@starthinker.mtv.corp.google.com>
Cc: Alexey Dobriyan <adobriyan@gmail.com>,
	Stephen Hemminger <shemminger@vyatta.com>,
	Ben Huchings <ben@decadent.org.uk>,
	"Stanislav O. Bezzubtsev" <stas@lvk.cs.msu.su>,
	linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org
To: "David S. Miller" <davem@davemloft.net>, netdev@vger.kernel.org
Return-path: <linux-kernel-owner@vger.kernel.org>
Sender: linux-kernel-owner@vger.kernel.org
List-Id: netdev.vger.kernel.org

The macros for the values of the bit field describing the four
different versions of statistics supported by different hardware
variants were being misused.  Where the code was trying to test if the
hardware implements V3, it was actually testing whether it implements
any of V1, V2, or V3, causing the driver to report statistics that
don't really exist in the hardware, with bogus values.

Signed-off-by: Mike Ditto <mditto@google.com>
---
This fix establishes two sets of macros, one for referring to versions
individually, and another for referring to the set of, e.g., V1, V2,
and V3 together.  This seemed less messy than expanding out V1|V2|V3
in all the device table entries.

This patch against v2.6.34 was tested on an MCP55 chip, on which the
problem had been observed.

 drivers/net/forcedeth.c |   60 ++++++++++++++++++++++++----------------------
 1 files changed, 31 insertions(+), 29 deletions(-)

